// Local Google Calendar OAuth credentials for MeetingBarNG.
//
// This lets you use your OWN Google Calendar in a local build without
// committing secrets. To enable it:
//
//   1. Copy this file to `GoogleSecrets.xcconfig` (same folder). That copy is
//      git-ignored, so your credentials are never committed. `Project.xcconfig`
//      includes it automatically when present and it overrides the
//      REPLACE_BY_YOUR_* placeholders.
//
//   2. In Google Cloud Console (https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials):
//        - Create/select a project and enable the "Google Calendar API".
//        - Configure the OAuth consent screen (User type: External). While the
//          app is unverified, add your own Google account under "Test users",
//          or you won't be able to sign in.
//        - Create an "OAuth client ID". Two client types work with this app's
//          reversed-domain redirect (com.googleusercontent.apps.<CLIENT_NUMBER>):
//
//            * iOS  (recommended): no client secret. Bundle ID: leits.MeetingBar.
//                                  Leave GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET empty below.
//            * Desktop app:        has a client secret; put it in
//                                  GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET below.
//
//   3. From the generated Client ID (e.g. 1234567890-abc123.apps.googleusercontent.com),
//      GOOGLE_CLIENT_NUMBER is everything BEFORE ".apps.googleusercontent.com".
//      The app appends the suffix for the client ID and builds the redirect
//      scheme com.googleusercontent.apps.<GOOGLE_CLIENT_NUMBER> for you.
//
//   4. GOOGLE_AUTH_KEYCHAIN_NAME is any stable string; it names the Keychain
//      item that stores your OAuth tokens.
//
//   5. Rebuild (make build). Then pick "Google Calendar" in onboarding.

GOOGLE_CLIENT_NUMBER = 1234567890-abc123example
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET =
GOOGLE_AUTH_KEYCHAIN_NAME = MeetingBarNG.GoogleAuth
